  "explanation": {
    "id": "d497a7cf-385d-45e0-85fc-557dd6a943e6",
    "body": "The explanation states that $\\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty} \\frac{e^{k}}{3 k}$ is convergent by the ratio test since $\\lim_{k\\to\\infty}\\frac{\\frac{e^{k+1}}{3(k+1)}}{\\frac{e^k}{3k}}=\\frac{e}{3}$. There was a minor calculation error for this series. $\\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty} \\frac{e^{k}}{3 k}$ is actually divergent by the ratio test as $\\lim_{k\\to\\infty}\\frac{\\frac{e^{k+1}}{3(k+1)}}{\\frac{e^k}{3k}}=e$ rather than $\\frac{e}{3}$. The overall answer to this question is still correct as the ratio test does in fact give an inconclusive answer for $\\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty} \\frac{k+3}{5 k-1}$",
    "questionId": "e28b4970-4e2a-4b09-806e-af68199f93d5",
